// OsImportSlot — the OS primitives a dynamically-linked target reaches through an import
// table, and the slot index a call site records for its writer to patch.
//
// ⭐ THE SLOT-INDEX MODEL IS OS-NEUTRAL; ONLY THE SYMBOL SPELLING IS NOT. A call site emits
// "call through import slot k" and the object writer resolves k to an address: the Mach-O
// writer against a `__got` entry dyld binds, the PE writer against an IAT entry (`IatSlot`,
// the identical shape). Nothing about `k` is Mach-O, which is why this enum does NOT belong
// in `Targets/Macos/` — two files under `Targets/Arm64/` (the isel and the entry stub) named
// a Mach-O type to say "the exit primitive", a layering leak that only became visible when
// arm64-linux arrived and needed the same three primitives with NO import table at all.
//
// The per-format SYMBOL map stays with its writer (`machoImportSymbol` renders the Mach-O
// ABI's leading underscore: a C `exit` is the symbol `_exit`), because that IS format
// knowledge. Adding an import is still: append a case here and its symbol there.
//
// ⚠ arm64-LINUX USES NONE OF THIS. Linux permits raw syscalls, so `osExit`/`osWriteStdout`/
// `osAllocPages` lower to `svc #0` and the ELF image carries no import table, no GOT and no
// dynamic linker (see `Arm64Runtime.arm64OsCallKind`). The enum is reached only on the
// import-table route.
//
// `exit` terminates the process with main's return code. `write` is both the panic runtime's
// route to stderr and the `osWriteStdout`/`print` primitive. `mmap` is the page source
// `osAllocPages` bottoms out in; MAP_ANON returns ZEROED pages, satisfying the slab's zeroing
// contract. `munmap` releases such a mapping (`osFreePages`) and `madvise` drops its physical
// backing while KEEPING the mapping (`osDecommitPages`).
//
// ⛔ **THIS LIST SAID "No `munmap`: the slab never decommits, so a page-free import would be dead",
// AND THAT SENTENCE OUTLIVED ITS PREMISE.** It was true of the bump heap, which never frees; it was
// already false for the green-thread scheduler, which releases a completed GT's stack — that op just
// happened to be x64-windows-gated, so the missing import surfaced as a PANIC in the arm64 isel rather
// than as a link error here. ⚠ Only arm64-macOS actually reaches these two: the two Linux lanes make the
// same calls as raw `svc`/`syscall` and need no import table at all (see the ⚠ note above), which is why
// two names appear here for four lanes.
// ⚠ APPENDED, never inserted: `osImportSlotIndex` is the enum ORDINAL and every emitted `arm64ImportCall`
// records it, so a reorder would silently repoint every existing call site.
//
// ⚠ There is no `mprotect` slot, and that is not an omission: POSIX has no COMMIT state to change a
// protection into, so `osCommitPages` is an `mmap` of the sub-range (`MAP_FIXED`) and reaches the `mmap`
// slot already listed — exactly as `osReservePages` does. One page source, three spellings of its arguments:
// allocate, reserve, commit.
export enum OsImportSlot
	exit
	write
	mmap
	munmap
	madvise
	// --- the arm64-macOS host surface (`Targets/Arm64/Arm64DarwinRuntime`) ---
	//
	// ⚠ **THESE ARE REACHED FROM ONE LANE ONLY, AND THE ENUM IS STILL THE RIGHT HOME.** Every slot above is
	// reached by arm64-macOS alone too (the Linux lanes make the same calls as raw `svc`), so "only one lane
	// uses it" was never this list's membership rule — being an OS primitive reached through an IMPORT
	// TABLE is. What a Linux lane needs is a syscall NUMBER, which is a different fact in a different file.
	//
	// `clock_gettime_nsec_np` answers a Darwin clock in nanoseconds directly in x0 — the calendar, the
	// monotonic counter and this thread's CPU time are one import asked with three different `clockid_t`s,
	// which is why three facilities cost one slot. `nanosleep` is the timed park.
	clockGettimeNsecNp
	nanosleep
	// `sysconf` answers a numbered system parameter in x0 — `osCpuCount` asks it
	// `_SC_NPROCESSORS_ONLN`. ⚠ **IT IS THE FIRST SLOT HERE WITH NO LINUX TWIN AT ALL**, where every slot
	// above merely reaches its twin as a raw `svc`: Linux serves this parameter through a libc FUNCTION and
	// numbers it differently, so there is no syscall number to pair with this slot even in principle
	// (`StdToArm64Conversion.DarwinScNprocessorsOnln` carries the numbers and their provenance — do not
	// restate them here). That is why `targetProvidesFacility` gives `cpuCount` to arm64-macOS alone, and why
	// `lowerOsCpuCount` asks `arm64OsCallKind` before emitting a call through this slot.
	sysconf
	// --- MAC2: the `processInfo` facility's six libSystem entries ---
	//
	// ⚠ **SIX SLOTS FOR THREE OPS, AND THE ARITHMETIC IS THE POINT.** Windows answers each of these
	// questions with ONE import (`GetModuleFileNameA`, `GetCurrentProcessId`, `SetPriorityClass` +
	// `GetPriorityClass`); Darwin needs a second call for two of them, because `_NSGetExecutablePath`
	// answers *"a path"* rather than *"the real path"* (its own header says so — see
	// `Arm64DarwinRuntime.buildArm64DarwinExePathChunk`, which carries the measurement) and `setpriority`
	// answers nothing at all. That extra call is the whole reason the executable path is a CHUNK here and
	// an isel line on Windows.
	//
	// `_NSGetExecutablePath` is the Darwin image-path primitive; `realpath` canonicalizes what it answers
	// and `malloc`s the result, which `free` releases. `getpid` is the process id. `setpriority` and
	// `getpriority` are the two halves of `osEnterBackgroundPriority` — a SET that reports nothing and the
	// READ-BACK that is the op's answer.
	//
	// ⚠ **NONE OF THE SIX HAS A LINUX TWIN THAT THIS ENUM COULD SERVE**, for `sysconf`'s reason one line
	// up and one stronger: Linux reads its own image path from the `/proc/self/exe` SYMLINK (a `readlink`
	// on a path, not a call that answers one) and scopes `setpriority` to the THREAD rather than to the
	// process. So a Linux lane re-answers the question rather than re-spelling this call, which is why
	// `targetProvidesFacility` gives `processInfo` to arm64-macOS alone.
	nsGetExecutablePath
	realpath
	free
	getpid
	setpriority
	getpriority
	// --- MAC3: the green-thread floor's libSystem substrate ---
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **FIFTEEN SLOTS BECAUSE WIN32 ANSWERS THIS FAMILY WITH KERNEL OBJECTS AND POSIX ANSWERS IT WITH
	// STRUCTS THE CALLER OWNS.** `CreateEventA`/`SetEvent`/`WaitForSingleObject` are three imports over an
	// opaque HANDLE; the POSIX shape of the same auto-reset event is a counting condition variable — a mutex,
	// a condvar and a count in memory the program allocates — which is `pthread_mutex_*` + `pthread_cond_*` +
	// `malloc`. `Arm64DarwinRuntime`'s `DarwinWaitObject` carries the layout and Go's `semasleep`/`semawakeup`
	// the shape.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **NOT `dispatch_semaphore`, AND THIS PROJECT HAS THE SCAR.** A Mach-port-backed semaphore is not
	// async-signal-safe and wedged processes in macOS's unkillable `UE` state on a kill — v1 says so at
	// `Arm64MacosGreenThread.maxon:2203-2205`, and `feedback`/`project_arm64_macos_wedge_fix` records the same
	// failure from the other direction (the fix there was dispatch_semaphore → pthread cond/mutex). A condvar
	// is the shape both references converged on.
	//
	// `pthread_key_create` reserves the per-OS-thread slot the scheduler publishes its P in and
	// `pthread_setspecific` writes it; there is deliberately NO `pthread_getspecific`, because the READ is a
	// thread-pointer + indexed load with no call at all (`StdToArm64Conversion.lowerTlsSlotLoad`, which
	// carries the measurement of Darwin's TSD layout). `pthread_create`/`pthread_join` are the worker M's
	// birth and death. `getenv` answers `MAXON_MAX_PROCS`. `malloc` allocates a wait object, and `free` — five
	// slots up, MAC2's — releases one.
	//
	// ⚠ **`pthread_cond_timedwait_relative_np` RATHER THAN `pthread_cond_timedwait`**, and the `_np` is the
	// point: the portable call takes an ABSOLUTE deadline on the condvar's clock, so using it would mean
	// reading that clock first and would make the wait's correctness depend on which clock the condvar was
	// initialised against. The relative form takes the timeout the op already carries.
	//
	// ⚠ **THERE IS NO `sigaction`/`sigaltstack` SLOT, AND THAT IS A SCOPE STATEMENT RATHER THAN AN OMISSION.**
	// An alternate signal stack is only reachable through `SA_ONSTACK`, which is a flag on a HANDLER — and
	// this backend installs none (`Arm64Runtime`'s header: arm64 defines `sdiv`-by-0 as 0 and has no fault
	// entry on either OS). Adding the two imports with no handler to flag would be dead weight; a fault on a
	// green thread's stack dies as an unhandled signal here exactly as a fault on the main stack already does,
	// and closing that is the arm64 fault-handler rung's, not this one's.
	malloc
	pthreadKeyCreate
	pthreadSetspecific
	pthreadMutexInit
	pthreadMutexLock
	pthreadMutexUnlock
	pthreadMutexDestroy
	pthreadCondInit
	pthreadCondSignal
	pthreadCondWait
	pthreadCondTimedwaitRelativeNp
	pthreadCondDestroy
	pthreadCreate
	pthreadJoin
	getenv
	// ⭐⭐ **THE THREE ATTRIBUTE CALLS EXIST BECAUSE A WIN32 `CRITICAL_SECTION` IS RECURSIVE AND A DEFAULT
	// POSIX MUTEX IS NOT — MEASURED, as a HANG.** `osLockInit`'s Std-tier callers take the same lock nested
	// (`SchedRuntime`'s own header records it: *"a nested `LeaveCriticalSection` releases nothing"*), which
	// `EnterCriticalSection` permits by definition. A `pthread_mutex_init(m, NULL)` mutex is
	// `PTHREAD_MUTEX_DEFAULT` = `PTHREAD_MUTEX_NORMAL`, whose second lock by the owning thread DEADLOCKS: the
	// first arm64-macOS `async` build hung in `__slab_refill` → `_pthread_mutex_firstfit_lock_wait` on the
	// slab's own re-entered lock. So the region `osLockInit` initialises is built with
	// `PTHREAD_MUTEX_RECURSIVE`, and these are the calls that say so.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE WAIT OBJECT'S OWN MUTEX IS DELIBERATELY *NOT* RECURSIVE**, and the difference is not tidiness:
	// `pthread_cond_wait` on a recursively-held mutex releases ONE level, so a condvar paired with a recursive
	// mutex is undefined the moment anything nests it. That mutex is taken only by `Arm64DarwinRuntime`'s own
	// chunks, never nested, so it stays `NULL`-attributed and the condvar stays sound.
	pthreadMutexattrInit
	pthreadMutexattrSettype
	pthreadMutexattrDestroy
	// --- MAC4/5/6/7: the filesystem, standard-input and argv substrate ---
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **`__error` IS THE WHOLE errno STORY, AND IT IS A FUNCTION RATHER THAN A LOCATION.** POSIX makes
	// `errno` a per-thread lvalue; Darwin implements it as `*__error()`, so both READING the last failure and
	// PUBLISHING one go through this call. `Arm64DarwinRuntime.buildArm64DarwinLastErrorChunk` is the single
	// place that maps a Darwin errno onto the Win32 number `StdOp.osLastError`'s consumers test, which is why
	// the family costs one slot and not one per failing call.
	//
	// ⚠ **`open` IS THE READ HALF AND `creat` THE WRITE HALF, AND THAT PAIRING AVOIDS A VARIADIC CALL.**
	// `open(path, oflag, ...)` takes its permission bits as a VARARG, which AAPCS64 passes on the STACK — so
	// a create through `open` would need a frame this lowering does not have. `creat(path, mode)` is
	// `O_CREAT|O_TRUNC|O_WRONLY` with a NAMED mode parameter and is exactly what `CreateFileA(GENERIC_WRITE,
	// CREATE_ALWAYS)` means, so the two opens are two non-variadic calls instead of one variadic one.
	// `openWriteExecutable` is the same call with 0755 rather than 0644 — see `StdOp.osFileOpenWriteExecutable`
	// for why that is its own op rather than a flag on `osFileOpenWrite`.
	//
	// `read`/`close` complete the file handle's life (`write` is five slots up — the same descriptor write
	// `osWriteStdout` already reaches, because a descriptor is an argument and not a symbol). `fsync` is the
	// write-through half of `MOVEFILE_WRITE_THROUGH`, applied to the DESTINATION DIRECTORY after a `rename`,
	// which is where POSIX records a rename's durability.
	//
	// `stat`/`fstat`/`fstatat` are the three spellings the find-data record is filled from: by PATH for a
	// non-glob search and for `GetFileAttributesA`, by DESCRIPTOR for `GetFileSizeEx`, and RELATIVE TO AN
	// OPEN DIRECTORY for each entry of a walk (which is what `dirfd` exists for — it means the walk never
	// concatenates a path and never owns a path buffer).
	//
	// `opendir`/`readdir`/`closedir` + `fnmatch` are `FindFirstFileA`'s emulation: Win32 takes a GLOB and
	// POSIX takes a DIRECTORY, so the pattern is split at its last `/`, the prefix is opened and every entry
	// is matched against the suffix. `unlink`, `rename`, `mkdir` and `getcwd` are the four path operations
	// with a one-to-one Win32 twin.
	errnoLocation
	open
	creat
	read
	close
	fsync
	stat
	fstat
	fstatat
	unlink
	rename
	mkdir
	getcwd
	opendir
	readdir
	closedir
	dirfd
	fnmatch
	// --- MAC8: the child-process substrate ---
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THIRTEEN SLOTS BECAUSE `CreateProcessA` IS ONE CALL AND `posix_spawn` IS A BUILDER.** Win32
	// describes a child with two STRUCTS the caller fills (`STARTUPINFOA`, `SECURITY_ATTRIBUTES`) and one
	// call that reads them; POSIX describes it with an OPAQUE OBJECT built by a sequence of calls — a
	// `posix_spawn_file_actions_t` told, one call at a time, which descriptor becomes which, and a
	// `posix_spawnattr_t` told which flags apply. That is the whole reason for the count.
	//
	// ⚠ **`posix_spawnP` AND NOT `posix_spawn`, AND THE `p` IS THE PATH SEARCH.** `stdlib/Subprocess.maxon`'s
	// `resolveByName` documents its own fallback in so many words — *"on POSIX the runtime falls back to
	// execvp"* — because `subprocessResolveOnPath` is allowed to miss and hand the BARE NAME on. The
	// non-searching form would turn that documented miss into a spawn failure.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THERE IS NO `fcntl` SLOT, AND ITS ABSENCE IS A DESIGN DECISION RATHER THAN AN OMISSION.** The
	// descriptors a child must NOT inherit are closed by `POSIX_SPAWN_CLOEXEC_DEFAULT`
	// (`Arm64DarwinRuntime.DarwinSpawnCloexecDefault`), which inverts the POSIX default so that ONLY what
	// the file actions name survives the exec — precisely what Win32's `bInheritHandles` + `STARTUPINFOA`
	// pair means. Marking each end `FD_CLOEXEC` by hand would need `fcntl`, whose third argument is a
	// VARARG that Darwin's arm64 ABI passes on the STACK — a frame these chunks do not have.
	//
	// ⚠ **`poll` IS THE PEEK, AND THERE IS NO `ioctl`/`FIONREAD` SLOT FOR THE SAME VARARG REASON.**
	// `PeekNamedPipe` answers a byte COUNT; `mrt_darwin_pipe_peek` answers only READABLE-or-not and lets the
	// following `read` report the true count, which is all the drain loop uses the answer for.
	//
	// `pipe` is the anonymous pair Win32 spells as a uniquely-named named pipe. `waitpid` is
	// `WaitForSingleObject` + `GetExitCodeProcess` in one call — POSIX fuses "has it exited?" with "what did
	// it answer?", which is why a child's handle on this lane is an OBJECT that caches the reaped status
	// rather than a bare pid (see `DarwinWaitKindProcess`). `kill` is `TerminateProcess`. `_NSGetEnviron`
	// answers the address of `environ`, which a Mach-O main executable may not name directly.
	pipe
	poll
	waitpid
	kill
	posixSpawnp
	posixSpawnFileActionsInit
	posixSpawnFileActionsAdddup2
	posixSpawnFileActionsAddchdirNp
	posixSpawnFileActionsDestroy
	posixSpawnattrInit
	posixSpawnattrSetflags
	posixSpawnattrDestroy
	nsGetEnviron
	// --- Is standard output a TERMINAL? ---
	//
	// ⭐ **ONE SLOT FOR THE WHOLE `terminalDetection` FACILITY, AND IT HAS NO LINUX TWIN EITHER.** `isatty` is
	// a libc FUNCTION rather than a syscall — its body is `ioctl(fd, TCGETS, &termios)` and a status test — so
	// a libc-less Linux image would make that call itself and reach nothing here, exactly as `sysconf` and the
	// six `processInfo` entries above it are reached by one lane only.
	isatty
end 'OsImportSlot'

// The import slot index a call site records and the writer patches against. One accessor
// over the enum, exactly like `iatSlotIndex`.
export function osImportSlotIndex(slot OsImportSlot) returns RelocSlotIndex
	return slot.ordinal as RelocSlotIndex
end 'osImportSlotIndex'
